Subject: syscall, net: treat the host calls that can wait as system calls on
 OS threads
Last-Update: 2026-10-04

On Linux a system call is bracketed by the runtime's entersyscall and
exitsyscall: the goroutine's P is marked, so that sysmon can hand it to
another thread if the call is long and a stop-the-world can take it at
once, since the goroutine touches no Go memory until the call returns.
The wasip1 port makes its WASI preview 1 calls bare, which costs nothing
on one thread, where there is no other thread to hand a P to and no
collection runs concurrently.

With OS threads a thread blocked in a host call held its P: the
collector's stop-the-world waited for the call to return, and every
other thread with it, so a program with one goroutine in a read of a
pipe stopped at its first collection. With GOWASM=threads the calls that
can wait are bracketed as on Linux: the file layer's, the socket calls
that wait for a peer (accept, connect, receive and send) and the
resolver's. Their arguments are evaluated before the bracket, since no
Go call may fall inside it. The single-threaded builds keep the bare
calls, through empty hooks. The port's nanotime1 is nosplit, as every
other port's clock is: casgstatus reads the clock while a goroutine
leaves the syscall state, where a stack split is not allowed.

diff --git a/src/net/cgo_wasix.go b/src/net/cgo_wasix.go
index 8059740..3576ace 100644
--- a/src/net/cgo_wasix.go
+++ b/src/net/cgo_wasix.go
@@ -19,6 +19,16 @@ const cgoAvailable = true
 //go:noescape
 func wasix_resolve(host unsafe.Pointer, hostLen uint32, port int32, addrs unsafe.Pointer, naddrs uint32, ret unsafe.Pointer) syscall.Errno
 
+// A lookup waits on the system's resolver, so the call is a system call
+// to the scheduler, as a cgo lookup is on Linux: with OS threads the P
+// is handed on or taken by a stop-the-world meanwhile.
+//
+//go:linkname runtime_entersyscall runtime.entersyscall
+func runtime_entersyscall()
+
+//go:linkname runtime_exitsyscall runtime.exitsyscall
+func runtime_exitsyscall()
+
 // wasixAddrOf is WASIX's addr: the family tag, a padding byte and the
 // address octets.
 type wasixAddrOf struct {
@@ -34,7 +44,10 @@ func wasixLookupIP(name string) ([]IPAddr, error) {
 	var addrs [32]wasixAddrOf
 	var n uint32
 	host := []byte(name)
-	errno := wasix_resolve(unsafe.Pointer(&host[0]), uint32(len(host)), 0, unsafe.Pointer(&addrs[0]), uint32(len(addrs)), unsafe.Pointer(&n))
+	hostPtr := unsafe.Pointer(&host[0])
+	runtime_entersyscall()
+	errno := wasix_resolve(hostPtr, uint32(len(host)), 0, unsafe.Pointer(&addrs[0]), uint32(len(addrs)), unsafe.Pointer(&n))
+	runtime_exitsyscall()
 	if errno != 0 {
 		return nil, &DNSError{Err: errno.Error(), Name: name, IsNotFound: errno == syscall.ENOENT}
 	}
